Z||000000||johnt671||03-18-2001||06:15 PM||||i was trying to check the fuseable link for the fuel pump as the pump isn't coming on until the oil pressure lite goes out.  book said this means the relay or the link is bad. as i traced the wire to the starter, i found the connection loose on the starter.  i can wiggle it with my fingers.  i put a wrench on it and i couldn't get the nut to turn and looking at it closer it looks like the stud is coming out of the solenoid. starter is only a year old.  any ideas how i can get it to tighten or get it off?  84 4cyl.  thanks<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-18-2001||06:48 PM||||Hey John!<BR>If I am reading your post right, the stud is loose in the bakelite housing...<BR>This is typical for the GM starter, as the stud gets used...<BR>Sometimes you can remove the cable nut and tighten down the stud nut, but be careful!!!<BR>I have cracked the housing on more than one solenoid, as the heat from exhaust bakes it to death...<BR>Usually I replace the solenoid and add a heat shield (soup can) and the trouble stops...<BR>If I were you, I would eliminate any extra wires running to the starter, and run them to the battery...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||03-18-2001||07:29 PM||||If you have a Dremel tool with a cut off wheel disk, you may be able to cut a slot for a screwdriver in the bolt end to hold the bolt steady as you loosen the nut.||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||03-19-2001||07:03 PM||||thanks guys.  i want to getit off and save the started as it's less then a year old. on the other hand its an auto zone life time warrantee.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
